          <div class="life-verse">
            <blockquote>&ldquo;And they overcame him by the blood of the Lamb, and by the word of their testimony; and they loved not their lives unto the death.&rdquo;</blockquote>
            <cite>&mdash; Revelation 12:11 (KJV) &mdash; The overcomers&rsquo; weapon: their testimony</cite>
          </div>

          <div class="life-verse">
            <blockquote>&ldquo;Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy &hellip; the seventh day is the sabbath of the LORD thy God &hellip; For in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is, and rested the seventh day: wherefore the LORD blessed the sabbath day, and hallowed it.&rdquo;</blockquote>
            <cite>&mdash; Exodus 20:8&ndash;11 (KJV) &mdash; The commandment Bates spent his last dollar defending</cite>
          </div>

          <p class="life-body">
            Joseph Bates was not a polished theologian. He was a sea captain &mdash; a man accustomed to navigating by
            fixed stars and obeying fixed laws. When he read the Bible with the same precision he had applied to
            navigation charts, he could not escape the fourth commandment. He could not find Sunday in Scripture.
            He found only <strong>Saturday &mdash; the seventh day &mdash; blessed and hallowed by God Himself</strong>
            at the creation of the world.
          </p>
          <p class="life-body">
            What makes the autobiography so powerful is the <em>sequence</em>. Bates did not arrive at Sabbath
            truth by accident. He was systematically stripped of every worldly comfort &mdash; alcohol, tobacco, tea,
            coffee, meat, money, social standing &mdash; before God opened the Sabbath light to him. The autobiography
            shows that total reformation preceded total revelation. The pattern God used with Bates is the same pattern
            He uses today.
          </p>

